/T (Across the group, the pattern was clear: people responded strongly to the interface finally looking like it belonged to BCIT, and to small usability choices like chat over email. The one recurring note wasn't about the redesign's direction; it was smaller points of polish, colour choices, optional features, the kind of feedback that confirms the bigger decisions were already working.)
/E (Across the group, the pattern was clear: people responded strongly to the interface finally looking like it belonged to BCIT, and to small usability choices like chat over email. The one recurring note wasn't about the redesign's direction; it was smaller points of polish, colour choices, optional features, the kind of feedback that confirms the bigger decisions were already working.)
