1264 0 obj
<<
/Title (Accessibility came from a similar place: listening rather than assuming. Testing surfaced something I hadn't fully anticipated: several participants specifically needed larger text and stronger contrast to comfortably use the platform. Once that came up, I checked the colour pairings against WCAG contrast guidelines directly, rather than relying on gut feeling, since "looks fine to me" isn't the same as actually meeting a real accessibility standard. That wasn't a nice-to-have I added at the end; it reshaped earlier decisions about type scale and colour pairing well before the visual design was finalized.)
/Dest [24 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1259 0 R
>>
endobj
1265 0 obj
<<
/Title (Every stage fed the next one directly. Brand research told me what I couldn't change: the colours, the sense of institutional trust BCIT already had. Sketching let me test navigation ideas quickly, before getting attached to anything. Low-fidelity wireframes were where the click-reduction goal actually got tested for the first time, and multiple rounds of high fidelity in Figma were less about polish and more about making sure the accessibility changes held up once real content and spacing were in play, not just in a rough sketch. Nothing here was decorative; every stage existed because the previous one revealed something that needed solving.)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1260 0 R
>>
endobj
